A||||4||onfire||Drop spindles or lowering springs?||||5||
Z||000000||onfire||09-24-2001||02:35 AM||||I bought the wheels my friend had on his Fiero. 17x7 American Racing Pinzetti's with 215 40's on them. Well it needs to be lowered. I just wanted some opinions on drop spindles or springs. I would either go with <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/dropspindles.html" TARGET=_blank>Street Dreams</A> drop spindles, or Eibach lowering springs. Pros and cons of each setup. I know if I get the drop spindles I would still have to get lowering springs for the rear, or maybe coil overs. I plan on doing the 11.25" big brake conversion also. So would the drop spindles work with these brakes? I don't see why they wouldn't, but I am not exactly ASE certified either. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So let me know what you think. Pics. of the wheels to come.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-24-2001||03:37 AM||||If you can afford it, dropped spindles is definitely the way to go.  That's the only way I know to keep the stock steering/suspension geometry.  I'd also buy some new, higher rate front springs and coil overs for the rear.  Of course, new shocks and struts, too.  The new front springs aren't just for performance.  After 15 years, the original springs are worn (at least a little), although I haven't heard of anyone having problems with dropped spindles and the original springs.  This is the setup I'm planning on going with whenever I can afford it.  Working on the interior now so I can't afford any suspension work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||onfire||09-24-2001||01:03 PM||||Thanks for the response. I am still undecided on what to do. I want the big brakes and suspension done by Jan. so I can start on the engine(performance). So does anyone else have any thoughts about suspension?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||VISCERAL||09-24-2001||03:27 PM||||Ask tigger, he has the dropped spindles on the front of his orange car. For the back go for the <A HREF="http://www.groundcontrol.com" TARGET=_blank>www.groundcontrol.com</A>  rear coilover kit. except don't let them give you the 8" length spring, get the 10" eibach 275-300lb springs. The 8" likes to bottom out too much on my car. LT1fiero has 10" and he says they don't bottom out like his old 8" used to.<BR>get kyb's and if you can afford them get konis from <A HREF="http://www.shox.com---good" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com---good</A>  prices or <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.summitracing.com</A>     good luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChopTop||09-24-2001||03:55 PM||||Go with dropped spindles if you can.  I have Bell Tech dropped spindles, polyurethane bushings all around and the original GT front springs with lowering springs on rear (Bilstein shocks & struts) on my 85GT.  On my 85SE I'm running Suspension Techniques lowering springs, polyurethane bushings and Monroe Sensatrack shocks and struts.  No comparison, the GT rides so much better than the SE, and I believe it's due mainly to the DS. ||||reg||1
